  "body": "### Summary\nAs the maintainer, I want to decide whether the anonymizer should raise when a signature or mood is cut past its first balanced `</span>`. Today an unknown HFR element printed right before HFR's known tail is deleted silently with the block. This is #797 point 3's own example.\n\n### Facts\n- #794 (#763) runs a signature or mood to the last `</span>` before HFR's next element. That redacts a member's unbalanced markup on purpose: `…</span>Secret Handle<span class=\"x\">…`. Its tests pin this redaction.\n- PR #812 (#797) raises when anything other than HFR's own tail follows that `</span>`. An unknown HFR element followed directly by the known tail is still cut, for example the invented `<br/><span class=\"MDStatus\">…</span>` after a mood followed by `</td>` or the avatar. Byte for byte, it looks the same as the member shape above: members get classed spans from BBCode (`span.u`, `span.red`, …).\n- Nothing leaks: only HFR content is lost, and no corpus page has the shape.\n\n### Options\n1. Keep the current behaviour (documented in `tooling/fixtures/README.md`).\n2. Raise whenever the depth count and the boundary disagree. This closes the gap, but #794's unbalanced-markup captures would then need an extension before they can be published, and #794's redaction tests would become raise tests.\n\n### Acceptance\nThe operator picks an option. If option 2: tests first, then `check --corpus`, `manifest_check.py` and the tooling tests green.",
